## What is wrong

`subtest 'failed after three quick exits'` in `t/stdio.t` counts on a timing
window: three spawn-and-handshake cycles have to fit into the `KILL_GRACE` of
5 seconds (`MCP::Hub::Upstream::Stdio`). Under load the three cycles took
5.25 s, so the third exit was not counted and the upstream ended up `stopped`
instead of `failed` (`got: 'stopped' expected: 'failed'`).

The subtest failed 1 in 3 in the full suite and never in isolation. It is not
a regression of the k17/k18/k19 batch: the same failure happens with the code
of `main` before the batch, and the old and the new library count the exits
identically (3 exits, 3 s).

## Reproduce

    cd p5-mcp-hub && prove -lr t/

fails about every third time when the machine is busy. In isolation:

    prove -l t/stdio.t

passes every time.

## Done

- The subtest does not depend on the wall clock: either the window is measured
  from the first exit, or the child is told to exit at once and the elapsed
  time is checked against a generous limit.
- The failure window and `KILL_GRACE` are not tested against each other: a test
  that needs three cycles in five seconds says more about the machine than
  about the code.

